1
00:00:00,530 --> 00:00:02,930
Our friend here is hiding
behind a partition,

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:05,275
and he is very pleased about it.

3
00:00:05,355 --> 00:00:06,835
That sensor can't see him.

4
00:00:06,915 --> 00:00:09,395
It's pointed at a plain, blank wall.

5
00:00:09,475 --> 00:00:14,835
And yet this is real data,
from a study published in 2026:

6
00:00:14,915 --> 00:00:17,875
seen from above, a small
sensor aimed at a wall,

7
00:00:17,955 --> 00:00:20,455
tracking someone it never saw directly.

8
00:00:21,035 --> 00:00:23,315
Here's the trick for
seeing around corners.

9
00:00:23,395 --> 00:00:25,568
The light doesn't go
through the partition.

10
00:00:25,648 --> 00:00:28,075
It goes around the end,
by way of the wall.

11
00:00:28,155 --> 00:00:30,982
The sensor fires a short, invisible flash.

12
00:00:31,062 --> 00:00:33,808
The wall scatters it, part
reaches the hidden person,

13
00:00:33,888 --> 00:00:37,475
and a tiny bit bounces
back: wall, then sensor.

14
00:00:37,555 --> 00:00:40,275
That trip is longer than a
quick bounce off the wall,

15
00:00:40,355 --> 00:00:42,168
so it arrives a little later.

16
00:00:42,248 --> 00:00:44,595
A few billionths of a second later.

17
00:00:44,675 --> 00:00:46,435
Your webcam can't time that.

18
00:00:46,515 --> 00:00:48,515
This takes a time-of-flight sensor,

19
00:00:48,595 --> 00:00:51,355
a camera that clocks its
own light's round trip.

20
00:00:51,435 --> 00:00:54,355
Light travels about thirty
centimetres in a nanosecond,

21
00:00:54,435 --> 00:00:56,195
one billionth of a second.

22
00:00:56,275 --> 00:01:00,895
So timing is distance, and the extra
delay is a clue to where he is.

23
00:01:01,310 --> 00:01:03,630
Why does a plain wall work at all?

24
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:04,990
Start with a mirror.

25
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:07,550
Light leaves a mirror at
the same angle it arrived,

26
00:01:07,630 --> 00:01:09,363
so the picture stays whole.

27
00:01:09,443 --> 00:01:13,210
Put a mirror here, and our
friend is simply visible.

28
00:01:13,350 --> 00:01:15,830
A painted wall is rough up close.

29
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:17,790
It throws light every which way,

30
00:01:17,870 --> 00:01:20,070
so each spot acts less like a mirror,

31
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:21,750
more like a tiny lamp.

32
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:25,190
You might picture his image as a
postcard shredded into confetti,

33
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:26,550
waiting to be sorted.

34
00:01:26,630 --> 00:01:27,990
It's worse than that.

35
00:01:28,070 --> 00:01:30,310
Confetti still holds bits of picture.

36
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:33,590
Everything coming back
is a blend of many paths.

37
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:35,970
What survives is timing.

38
00:01:36,115 --> 00:01:38,035
Follow the path that matters:

39
00:01:38,115 --> 00:01:42,915
sensor, wall, person, wall, sensor.

40
00:01:42,995 --> 00:01:44,675
Each bounce spreads the light,

41
00:01:44,755 --> 00:01:46,195
and most of it is lost.

42
00:01:46,275 --> 00:01:48,755
Nearly everything coming
back bounced once,

43
00:01:48,835 --> 00:01:49,875
off the wall.

44
00:01:49,955 --> 00:01:52,035
Our friend's echo bounced three times,

45
00:01:52,115 --> 00:01:53,005
so it's tiny.

46
00:01:53,085 --> 00:01:55,285
This is real data from the same team,

47
00:01:55,365 --> 00:01:57,805
with a different sensor and hidden object:

48
00:01:57,885 --> 00:02:01,645
the wall's big echo, then,
a few nanoseconds later,

49
00:02:01,725 --> 00:02:03,858
a bump you have to zoom in to see.

50
00:02:03,938 --> 00:02:06,925
In this capture, hundreds of times weaker.

51
00:02:07,005 --> 00:02:08,805
That bump is the clue.

52
00:02:08,885 --> 00:02:12,003
Its timing says how much
farther the light travelled.

53
00:02:12,115 --> 00:02:14,275
Let's turn timing into a map:

54
00:02:14,355 --> 00:02:16,395
the room from above, flattened,

55
00:02:16,475 --> 00:02:18,355
with one more simplification.

56
00:02:18,435 --> 00:02:21,715
The sensor flashes and listens
at one spot on the wall.

57
00:02:21,795 --> 00:02:25,555
Measure the extra delay, and you
know how far he is from that spot.

58
00:02:25,635 --> 00:02:26,915
Not which direction.

59
00:02:26,995 --> 00:02:28,195
Just how far.

60
00:02:28,275 --> 00:02:30,075
He could be anywhere on this arc,

61
00:02:30,155 --> 00:02:32,335
all the same distance from that spot.

62
00:02:32,645 --> 00:02:34,485
Now listen at a second spot.

63
00:02:34,565 --> 00:02:36,785
Another delay, another arc.

64
00:02:36,885 --> 00:02:40,865
On this side of the wall,
they cross in just one place.

65
00:02:41,335 --> 00:02:43,255
Measured timings are a bit fuzzy,

66
00:02:43,335 --> 00:02:45,215
so each arc is really a band,

67
00:02:45,295 --> 00:02:47,575
and the bands overlap in a small patch.

68
00:02:47,655 --> 00:02:51,175
These two spots are close, so
the patch is long and blurry.

69
00:02:51,255 --> 00:02:52,995
Spread them out, and it shrinks.

70
00:02:53,075 --> 00:02:56,435
In practice, a computer
weighs many spots at once,

71
00:02:56,515 --> 00:03:00,115
trying positions and keeping those
whose predicted echoes match.

72
00:03:00,195 --> 00:03:01,875
It also leans on assumptions,

73
00:03:01,955 --> 00:03:04,115
like what kind of object it's after.

74
00:03:04,195 --> 00:03:06,888
That's why the answer
is a likely location,

75
00:03:06,968 --> 00:03:08,195
or a rough shape.

76
00:03:08,275 --> 00:03:09,615
Not a photograph.

77
00:03:10,055 --> 00:03:11,815
None of this is brand new.

78
00:03:11,895 --> 00:03:16,975
In 2012, an MIT team reported recovering
the 3D shape of a small mannequin

79
00:03:17,055 --> 00:03:18,135
around a corner.

80
00:03:18,215 --> 00:03:21,415
It took an ultrafast laser
and a high-speed camera:

81
00:03:21,495 --> 00:03:23,315
lab equipment that filled a table.

82
00:03:23,395 --> 00:03:28,035
In a 2018 study, Stanford researchers
swept one spot across the wall,

83
00:03:28,115 --> 00:03:29,715
like our simplified picture.

84
00:03:29,795 --> 00:03:33,075
The math got simpler: for
a reflective exit sign,

85
00:03:33,155 --> 00:03:35,955
rebuilding the scene took
about a second on a laptop.

86
00:03:36,035 --> 00:03:38,835
Measuring still took almost seven minutes.

87
00:03:38,915 --> 00:03:43,795
By 2021, researchers in Wisconsin
and Milan had sped up measuring too:

88
00:03:43,875 --> 00:03:46,035
live video of ordinary objects,

89
00:03:46,115 --> 00:03:51,155
five frames a second, with a powerful
laser and custom detectors. Impressive.

90
00:03:51,235 --> 00:03:53,775
But all of it ran on research equipment.

91
00:03:53,950 --> 00:03:56,910
Then, in a study published in 2026,

92
00:03:56,990 --> 00:04:01,070
a team from MIT and Dartmouth tried
the small time-of-flight sensors,

93
00:04:01,150 --> 00:04:04,430
often called LiDAR, found
in phones and gadgets.

94
00:04:04,510 --> 00:04:06,430
Don't expect your phone to do this yet:

95
00:04:06,510 --> 00:04:09,350
phone makers often keep
the raw data private.

96
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:11,470
These sensors are tough customers.

97
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:13,950
Weak lasers mean fainter echoes.

98
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:17,310
The team's smartphone-grade
device had about a hundred pixels:

99
00:04:17,390 --> 00:04:19,150
a hundred listening spots.

100
00:04:19,230 --> 00:04:21,950
And if you hold one in
your hand, it jiggles.

101
00:04:22,030 --> 00:04:25,230
Their fix borrows night mode's
trick from phone cameras:

102
00:04:25,310 --> 00:04:29,070
stack many quick, dim frames
into one better estimate.

103
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:33,470
The catch: between frames, the
sensor jiggles and the person moves.

104
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:35,710
Plain averaging would smear everything,

105
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:37,825
like a long exposure of someone walking.

106
00:04:37,905 --> 00:04:39,985
Their method puts the motion to work,

107
00:04:40,065 --> 00:04:41,905
one unknown at a time.

108
00:04:41,985 --> 00:04:43,945
Move the sensor through known positions,

109
00:04:44,025 --> 00:04:45,985
and the listening spots spread out.

110
00:04:46,065 --> 00:04:50,305
Keep the sensor still, and each step
becomes a new position to follow.

111
00:04:50,385 --> 00:04:52,865
Our opening clip came
from a different sensor:

112
00:04:52,945 --> 00:04:56,305
an off-the-shelf kit the authors
put at under a hundred dollars,

113
00:04:56,385 --> 00:04:58,185
with sixteen listening spots,

114
00:04:58,265 --> 00:05:01,025
held still while a person
walked behind a partition.

115
00:05:01,105 --> 00:05:02,765
We ran it through their own code.

116
00:05:02,845 --> 00:05:04,605
Moved through known positions,

117
00:05:04,685 --> 00:05:09,145
the sensor behind that faint bump
rebuilt the rough outline of a hidden U.

118
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:11,290
Many of these tests had help:

119
00:05:11,370 --> 00:05:14,090
safety-vest style reflective
material on the target,

120
00:05:14,170 --> 00:05:16,490
which sends far more light straight back.

121
00:05:16,570 --> 00:05:19,370
Our clip's files don't say
if the walker wore any.

122
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:23,225
And the kit needs a flat wall and
a few seconds of empty room first.

123
00:05:23,305 --> 00:05:26,905
But the authors do report tracking
a person in ordinary clothes,

124
00:05:26,985 --> 00:05:29,785
with the sensor capturing
thirty frames a second.

125
00:05:29,865 --> 00:05:33,345
The code is public, though we've found
no other team reporting results on

126
00:05:33,425 --> 00:05:35,045
its own hardware yet.

127
00:05:35,465 --> 00:05:37,225
What might this be good for?

128
00:05:37,305 --> 00:05:40,665
Picture a delivery robot nearing
a blind warehouse corner.

129
00:05:40,745 --> 00:05:42,505
With a suitable wall at the junction,

130
00:05:42,585 --> 00:05:46,425
a sensor like this might give it an
early hint of movement out of sight.

131
00:05:46,505 --> 00:05:49,945
Just a fuzzy blob:
enough to say slow down,

132
00:05:50,025 --> 00:05:51,515
not enough to say who's there.

133
00:05:51,595 --> 00:05:54,715
And plenty is still hard: short range,

134
00:05:54,795 --> 00:05:57,675
dark or shiny walls, bright sunlight,

135
00:05:57,755 --> 00:05:59,995
and fast math on small hardware.

136
00:06:00,075 --> 00:06:03,115
The researchers call it
an early-stage prototype.

137
00:06:03,195 --> 00:06:05,475
No one has shown that
it prevents collisions.

138
00:06:05,555 --> 00:06:07,595
For now, it's a promising clue,

139
00:06:07,675 --> 00:06:09,255
not a safety system.

140
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:11,985
Which brings us back to our friend.

141
00:06:12,065 --> 00:06:15,172
Being out of sight isn't the
same as giving nothing away.

142
00:06:15,252 --> 00:06:16,692
The light found a way around,

143
00:06:16,772 --> 00:06:19,855
and careful timing and math
can read some of its clues.

144
00:06:19,935 --> 00:06:23,195
To really hide, he'd have
to block the bounces too.

145
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:31,870
This is Future Got Weird: the
strange future, explained.

146
00:06:31,950 --> 00:06:35,030
Subscribe for more, and we'll
see you around the corner.
